WEBVTT

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:24.359
Hymn really told me a lot about how to love and how to be a man, especially how to be a man, you know, growing up with a very religious toxic abusive father was very difficult for me and so I really had my emotions.
《老爸老妈浪漫史》真的教给我很多关于如何去爱以及如何做一个男人，尤其是如何做一个男人。你知道的，在一个极度宗教化、有毒且虐待我的父亲身边长大对我来说非常艰难，所以我真的封闭了自己的情感。

00:00:24.359 --> 00:00:54.433
I was thought it wasn't okay to cry, it wasn't okay to be vulnerable, but Ted taught me that and it was also being disabled, having cerebral palsy and autism, and he had taught me how to properly interact with people and how to read social cues and that stuff, so it made me become a better communicator, meaning become a better friend, it made me become a lover.
我曾经以为哭是不行的，脆弱是不行的，但泰德教会了我这些。而且作为一个残疾人，患有脑瘫和自闭症，他教我如何正确地与人交往，如何解读社交线索等等，这让我成为了一个更好的沟通者，意味着成为一个更好的朋友，成为了一个懂得去爱的人。

00:00:54.433 --> 00:01:00.232
Not just in the romantic sense but in a proper platonic sense as well.
不仅仅是在浪漫的意义上，在恰当的柏拉图式的精神恋爱意义上也是如此。

00:01:00.232 --> 00:01:07.026
So thank you Josh, thank you Carter, thank you Craig, thank you to the whole team.
所以谢谢乔什，谢谢卡特，谢谢克雷格，谢谢整个团队。

00:01:07.026 --> 00:01:13.819
I will continue watching a show for a little, because it's wait for it, legendary.
我会继续看一会儿这个节目，因为——等等看——太传奇了。

00:01:13.819 --> 00:01:15.001
I'm alone.
我孤身一人。

00:01:15.001 --> 00:01:16.892
I won't be soon.
但我很快就不会孤单了。

00:01:16.892 --> 00:01:21.147
You know, York City, when I see you.
你知道的，纽约城，当我见到你时。

00:01:21.147 --> 00:01:25.874
Please permit me to tell you everything.
请允许我向你倾诉一切。

00:01:25.874 --> 00:01:27.055
York City.
纽约城。

00:01:27.055 --> 00:01:27.555
Wow.
哇。

00:01:27.555 --> 00:01:37.955
Well, Alec, our producer warned us that was a crusher of a letter, and it certainly was.
好吧，亚力克，我们的制作人警告过我们那是一封重磅催泪弹，果真如此。

00:01:37.955 --> 00:01:44.337
What a, what an extraordinary thing to to let us know.
真是，真是件了不起的事，能让我们知道这些。

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:45.609
Thank you so much for that.
非常感谢你分享这个。

00:01:46.709 --> 00:01:47.670
I am Josh Ratner.
我是乔什·拉德纳。

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:59.150
Hello, welcome to another episode of How We Made Your Mother, where we discuss the television program, How I Met Your Mother, which ran from 2005 to 2014.
你好，欢迎收看另一期的《我们是如何创造你老爸老妈的》，在这里我们讨论从2005年播到2014年的电视节目《老爸老妈浪漫史》。

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:02.590
I played Ted Mosby on that show for all two underneath episodes.
在那部剧里，我在全部208集里都饰演了泰德·莫斯布。

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:09.310
The show was co-created by Carter Bays and my co-host, the beloved Craig Thomas.
这部剧是由卡特·贝斯和我的联合主持人、我挚爱的克雷格·托马斯共同创作的。

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:10.310
Hi, Craig.
嗨，克雷格。

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:11.590
Hey, Joshy.
嘿，乔什。

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:12.509
Boy, that one got me.
天哪，那封信真的触动了我。

00:02:12.509 --> 00:02:14.389
that lot of really, really, really got me.
那封信真的、真的、真的深深触动了我。

00:02:14.389 --> 00:02:18.469
I am so trying to recover to get it together, but I wanna be friends with that guy.
我正努力平复心情，把状态找回来，但我真的很想和那个家伙做朋友。

00:02:18.469 --> 00:02:21.189
I wanna be friends with him too.
我也想和他做朋友。

00:02:21.189 --> 00:02:23.389
He is our friend, we are friends.
他已经是我们的朋友了，我们是朋友。

00:02:23.389 --> 00:02:27.229
And thank you so much for sharing that experience.
非常感谢你分享这段经历。

00:02:27.229 --> 00:02:35.550
My God, I thought it would be our guest that would make me cry just having today's guest, but then Alec has to sucker punch us on the way in with this beautiful letter and now I'm like crying.
天哪，我以为今天的嘉宾会让我哭，只要有今天的嘉宾在就行，结果亚力克在开场时给我们当胸来了一拳，送上这封美丽的信，现在我都快哭了。

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:39.710
I thought simply having today's guest would burst into tears of joy.
我本以为仅仅迎来今天的嘉宾就会让我喜极而泣。

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:59.909
Well, we often have very special episodes of How We Made Your Mother, but today is a very, very, very, very extra, extra, extra, extra, extra special episode of How We Made Your Mother because we have the mother of all mothers, the director of the show, our beloved and forever friend Pam Friman is here.
好吧，我们的《我们是如何创造你老爸老妈的》经常有非常特别的单集，但今天是非常、非常、非常、非常、额外、额外、额外、额外、额外特别的一集，因为我们迎来了所有老妈的老妈、该剧的导演、我们挚爱且永远的朋友帕姆·弗莱曼。

00:02:59.909 --> 00:03:01.310
Hi, Pam.
嗨，帕姆。

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:04.270
Oh my gosh, thank you so much.
哦天哪，太感谢了。

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:08.870
Hi, friends, that was, it was perfection.
嗨，朋友们，那真是，太完美了。

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:09.486
believe it?
敢相信吗？

00:03:09.486 --> 00:03:10.495
It's so beautiful.
太美妙了。

00:03:10.495 --> 00:03:13.130
The best part of this is hearing from the fans.
这其中最棒的部分就是听到粉丝们的心声。

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:14.420
That's really the show.
这才是节目的真谛。

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:20.867
It's a way to just talk to connect with people like what we just heard and what an honor to hear that the show met.
这是一种与人们交流、建立联系的方式，就像我们刚刚听到的一样，听到这部剧达到了这样的效果是多么大的荣誉。

00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:22.437
All these years later, yeah.
历经这么多年，是的。

00:03:22.437 --> 00:03:31.687
And it really does feel like we made a thing and you make anything, it's like you put a bottle out in the ocean and you just hope someone gets the message inside it.
真的感觉就像是我们创造了一件作品，而当你创造任何东西时，就像是把一个漂流瓶扔进大海，你只是希望有人能收到里面的信息。

00:03:31.687 --> 00:03:41.610
And it really just feels like it belongs to the fans now and including, you know, there, there are all these theories, Pam, that we're hearing that we've asked Carter correctly.
而且真的感觉它现在属于粉丝了，包括，你知道的，有各种各样的理论，帕姆，我们听到后去向卡特求证。

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:42.955
Did you mean to do this?
你们是故意这么做的吗？

00:03:42.955 --> 00:03:45.366
Did you mean to do this color scheme thing?
你们是有意设计这种配色方案的吗？

00:03:45.366 --> 00:03:49.290
There's a whole conspiracy theory about the colors and what they mean.
有一个关于颜色及其含义的完整阴谋论。

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:57.420
And it's kind of like they didn't mean to, but they did on some subconscious level, you know, we're talking about dream logic here, right, Craig?
这有点像——虽然他们不是有意的，但他们在某种潜意识层面确实做到了，你知道的，我们这里讨论的是梦境逻辑，对吧，克雷格？

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:57.920
Yeah.
是的。

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.788
Yeah, there's something that comes out and like you hear crazy thing.
是的，有些东西自然而然地流露出来，就像你听到一些疯狂的理论。

00:04:01.788 --> 00:04:06.609
And then you're like, actually, there's something, there is something kind of to that.
然后你会想，其实，这里面确实有点道理。

00:04:07.349 --> 00:04:10.949
It's really, it's really cool that the show is like, is studied at that level.
这部剧能被研究到这种程度，真的很酷，真的很酷。

00:04:10.949 --> 00:04:13.541
And by the way, just to back it all the way up here.
顺便说一句，我们把话题拉回原点。

00:04:13.541 --> 00:04:21.116
Well, today's date, we'll talk a little bit about the episode and question that we're up to, which is Mary the Paralegal, which is really funny episode.
好吧，今天的日期，我们会稍微聊聊我们要讨论的那一集，也就是《女助手玛丽》，那真是有趣的一集。

00:04:21.116 --> 00:04:32.230
But we'll also just talk about Pam, like, oh my God, how we met Pam, like, Pam, one of the earliest believers in this thing, Carter and I were these two little boys, These two guys, they were in fifth grade when we met you.
但我们也会聊聊帕姆，哦天哪，我们是怎么遇到帕姆的，帕姆是这部作品最早的信徒之一，卡特和我当时还是两个小男孩，两个家伙，我们遇到你时还在读五年级。

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:36.269
Short little pennies, little beanies with propellers on them.
穿着短裤，戴着带小风扇的小帽。

00:04:36.269 --> 00:04:38.949
And we're like, would you read my script, little lady?
我们当时说：“小女士，你能读读我的剧本吗？”

00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:45.389
You're pretty and seem like an adult sitting in a Starbucks, just with the two.
你长得漂亮，看起来像个坐在星巴克里的大人，就我们两个。

00:04:45.389 --> 00:04:46.949
That is how we met Pam front.
这就是我们与帕姆·弗莱曼相遇的方式。

00:04:46.949 --> 00:04:48.750
And we heard the like the script.
然后我们听了剧本。

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:56.790
And I was told, by the way, I was told to go meet these two guys at Starbucks and I walked in and there were all these tables and there were two guys at each table.
顺便说一句，我当时被告知去星巴克见这两个家伙，我走进去，发现有好多桌子，每张桌子旁都有两个家伙。

00:04:56.790 --> 00:05:03.230
And I was like, what's your whole life could have changed if you went to the wrong table?
我在想，如果你走错了桌子，你的人生会发生多大的改变啊！

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:11.189
I know, they all had pilots to show you in Los Angeles that were written in that Starbucks.
我知道，他们在洛杉矶要给你看的试播集剧本都是在那家星巴克写的。

00:05:11.189 --> 00:05:14.269
Pam, tell us what your experience was.
帕姆，告诉我们你的经历是怎样的。

00:05:14.269 --> 00:05:17.259
Do you remember reading the pilot?
你还记得读试播集剧本的情景吗？

00:05:17.259 --> 00:05:18.259
Right.
没错。

00:05:18.259 --> 00:05:30.271
I certainly remember reading the pilot and it was unlike any other pilot and it was so funny but the heart got me, the heart got me.
我当然记得读试播集的情景，它与我读过的任何其他试播集都不同，它非常有趣，但真正打动我的是它的核心，是它展现的情感。

00:05:30.271 --> 00:05:35.731
And it was just a show I wanted to watch more than anything.
那是这是一部我极其渴望看到更多内容的剧集。

00:05:35.731 --> 00:05:51.110
So when the opportunity, then I was just lucky that they found me and that I got a chance to meet them and once you sit down with Carter and Craig, you're in, you're in.
所以当机会来临时，我很幸运他们找到了我，让我有机会见到他们。一旦你和卡特、克雷格坐下来，你就入伙了，你绝对入伙了。

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:58.870
Pam, one of the things we hear over and over again about the show, we're trying to uncover like, what is the enduring part of the show?
帕姆，关于这部剧，我们一遍又一遍听到的一件事——我们一直在试图找出这部剧经久不衰的秘诀究竟是什么？

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:03.189
And I think time and again, and we heard it in the opening, it's the heart part.
我发现一次又一次，我们在开场时也听到了，核心就是它的情感。

00:06:03.189 --> 00:06:13.870
And you, as the director, guarded that so religiously, I think that aspect and said, don't sell it all out to the jokes, don't make it too snarky.
而你作为导演，非常虔诚地守护着这一点，我认为正是这种特质，让你对大家说：“不要把所有筹码都押在笑话上，不要让它变得太刻薄。”

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:15.189
You let moments breathe.
你要给情绪留出呼吸的空间。

00:06:15.189 --> 00:06:23.149
Josh and I keep talking about this all the time about his performance on the show, like when Ashley Williams was on, we talked about their drum roll, please, like an episode like that.
乔什和我一直在谈论他在剧中的表现，比如阿什莉·威廉姆斯出演时，我们谈到了他们的倒计时（“倒数三二一”），诸如此类的一集。

00:06:23.149 --> 00:06:28.629
And there's certain episodes of the show where I go, I'm proud of how few jokes there are in this episode.
剧里总有那么几集，我会想：我为这一集里只有这么少笑话而感到骄傲。

00:06:28.629 --> 00:06:38.110
Meaning we let those emotional moments breathe, you seems to understand that that was the key to this show succeeding maybe before Carter and I did.
意思是，我们让那些情感时刻得以呼吸。你似乎比卡特和我更早明白，这是这部剧成功的最关键所在。

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:41.790
I don't know how much credit I can take.
我不知道自己能居功多少。

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:52.149
I just know what I loved and what resonated with me and with Josh Radner who put his hand on his heart and we once he did that.
我只知道我热爱什么，什么能与我以及乔什·拉德纳产生共鸣，他曾把手放在心口上，一旦他这样做——

00:06:52.149 --> 00:07:00.350
When he made that move once, that was like our sign to each other for the next 200 episodes.
当他做出过那次举动后，那就成了我们俩在接下来200多集里彼此的暗号。

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:08.389
Yeah, you know, it's funny, Pam, when you say it was a show you wanted to watch, I have this experience sometimes whether or something gets sent to me as an actor or a director.
是的，你知道的，很有趣，帕姆，当你说是你想看的剧时，我有时也会有这种经历，当有什么东西作为演员或导演送到我面前时。

00:07:08.389 --> 00:07:14.709
If I really love it, and there is that feeling like, I want to watch this, but I want to watch the best version of this.
如果我真的热爱它，就会有一种感觉：我想看这个，但我想要看这个的最完美版本。

00:07:14.709 --> 00:07:23.370
And I love it in such a way that I might be the person that is the right person to bring this to life because I feel the heartbeat of it.
我对它的爱如此深切，以至于我可能正是把这个故事带入现实的正确人选，因为我能感受到它的心跳。

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:29.930
And it's almost like you step in because you want to protect it or you want the best version of it to come to life.
这就像是你介入其中，因为你想保护它，或者你想让它的最佳版本呈现在世人面前。

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:36.089
Was there something like, because at that point, you know, we can talk a little about your career, but you had your pick of pilots, right?
当时有没有这样一种情况——因为在那之前，你知道的，我们可以稍微谈谈你的职业生涯，但你当时有很多试播集可供挑选，对吧？

00:07:36.089 --> 00:07:42.250
Like you were sending a ton of pilots, you could pick what you wanted to do and you landed on this one.
就像有大量试播集送到你手上，你可以挑选你想做什么，而你最终落脚在了这部。

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:47.660
And part of it, I sense is that you felt that pulse in it.
我感觉其中一部分原因，是你感受到了它内部的那次脉搏。

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:51.199
Well, I think that's all true.
嗯，我想这些都是真的。

00:07:51.199 --> 00:07:58.519
And one of the great things about this is that Carter and Craig were very young, hadn't done it a lot.
其中最棒的一点是，卡特和克雷格当时非常年轻，没有太多经验。

00:07:58.519 --> 00:08:03.519
And I knew that I could add some experience.
而我知道我可以贡献一些经验。

00:08:03.839 --> 00:08:06.240
I could help them sort of ship.
我可以帮他们掌舵。

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:09.079
I could be the adult in the room a little bit.
我可以稍微充当一下房间里的大人。

00:08:09.079 --> 00:08:12.560
And you guys, there were no rules.
而你们呢，当时根本没有规则。

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:19.240
You were just doing what you instinctively knew would be funny and would be beautiful.
你们只是在凭直觉做你们知道会很有趣、很美好的事情。

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:21.240
And we had craft service.
而且我们有剧组餐车。

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:27.519
You guys were more excited about that than I think any guy is all just, everything was just unbelievable.
比起任何一个家伙，你们俩对那件事的兴奋程度简直无以复加，一切都太不可思议了。

00:08:27.519 --> 00:08:28.680
We got free food.
我们有免费的食物。

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:31.500
I didn't know that was gonna be part of it.
我都不知道这也是福利的一部分。

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:41.460
And I wrote you this little day to send you in New York City.
我给你写了这个小纸条，派你去了纽约城。

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:51.820
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:54.179
And now, back to the show.
现在，言归正传。

00:08:54.179 --> 00:09:26.820
was there any negotiation between these guys are actually like we talked that that some of the brilliance of it is they didn't know any better like they didn't know you couldn't do x y z but you you had been around the world of sitcoms and didn't you i mean you apprenticed with james burrows right like that was part of your kind of education of coming up or but it feels like you You were standing on the shoulders of people who, you know, that kept moving the form forward.
有没有这样一种磨合：这些家伙实际上——就像我们聊过的，它的一些精妙之处在于他们初生牛犊不怕虎，他们不知道有些事是不能做的（比如X、Y、Z），但你一直在情景喜剧的世界里摸爬滚打，不是吗？我的意思是，你曾师从詹姆斯·伯罗斯，对吧？那是你早期教育的一部分，但感觉就像是你是站在巨人的肩膀上，是那些人不断推动这种艺术形式向前发展。

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:29.539
And I think that how I met your mother really did move the form forward.
而且我认为《老爸老妈浪漫史》确实推动了情景喜剧形式的发展。

00:09:29.539 --> 00:09:37.100
Was there any negotiation with, okay, these guys want to try to do something new, but also we have to honor the conventions of this thing at the same time?
这其中有没有过这样的协商与平衡：一方面这些家伙想尝试新东西，但另一方面我们同时又必须尊重这种形式的传统规范？

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:39.340
Was that ever attention?
这曾经是一种拉锯或矛盾吗？

00:09:39.340 --> 00:10:21.860
I think so, and I think we weren't focused on the something new as much as making the best version of what was created, and when we went to do it, we realized that we couldn't rely on what we had done forever, that it was written in such a specific and such a different in such a great way that we ended up taking four days to shoot the pilot without an audience and decided that if we got picked up, we'd never get picked up, but if we got picked up, we probably have an audience.
我也这么认为，我觉得我们当时并没有把重心放在标新立异上，而是专注于把创意的最佳版本呈现出来；当着手去做时，我们意识到我们不能永远依赖过去的经验，它的写作风格如此独特、与众不同又极为出色，以至于我们最终花了四天时间在没有观众的情况下拍摄了试播集，当时觉得要是被砍了也就被砍了，但如果能通过，我们大概还是得有现场观众。

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:34.259
And then when we finished and it was picked up, it was like we want to show that you shot and in order to give them that show, we had to sort of, we had to make it a hybrid.
而后来当我们完成并获得预订时，感觉就像是“我们要展示你拍的东西”，为了呈现出那种效果，我们不得不把它做成一种融合体。

00:10:34.259 --> 00:10:50.295
had to make it a multi-cam when it required it and make it a single-cam when it required it, stay inside, go outside, do all of these things that kind of blew up the convention of a multi-cam to serve how I met your mother.
在需要的时候做多机位拍摄，在需要的时候做单机位拍摄，留在室内，走到室外，做所有这些打破多机位常规的事情，来为《老爸老妈浪漫史》服务。

00:10:50.295 --> 00:10:52.740
And that's what made it so unique.
而这正是它如此独特的原因。

00:10:53.539 --> 00:10:55.348
Was that a tough negotiation?
那是一场艰难的谈判吗？

00:10:55.348 --> 00:11:01.084
Do you remember with the studio network to throw out the live audience in tape night format?
你还记得跟电视台和制片厂沟通取消现场观众和录像带之夜形式的事吗？

00:11:01.084 --> 00:11:08.379
Well, just to say, she had to explain it to me and Carter first, because Carter and I wrote this 70 page scene pilot.
嗯，应该这么说，她必须先向我和卡特解释，因为我和卡特写了一份70页场景的试播集剧本。

00:11:08.740 --> 00:11:11.659
And we're like, we could still basically do this in front of our audience with a few pre-tapes.
当时我们觉得，我们基本上还是可以在现场观众面前演这个，只要加几个预录片段就行。

00:11:11.659 --> 00:11:15.299
And like, Susie and Pam had to be like, let me explain how TV works to you guys.
结果苏茜和帕姆不得不对我们说：“让我来跟你们解释一下电视是怎么运作的吧。”

00:11:15.299 --> 00:11:16.139
Kids!
孩子们！

00:11:16.139 --> 00:11:17.139
Let me tell you something about television.
让我跟你们讲讲电视是怎么回事。

00:11:17.139 --> 00:11:18.460
It's in your little eyes, kids.
全写在你们那双小眼睛里了，孩子们。

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:20.700
They sat us on the couch with kids.
她们把我们按在沙发上，就像对待小孩子一样。

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:27.259
And there was like so clearly no way it could be what it was on the page and shoot the traditional way.
当时很明显，根本不可能按纸面上的剧本以传统方式拍摄。

00:11:27.259 --> 00:11:30.299
And you guys really had to explain that to us at a certain point.
在某个阶段，你们俩真的不得不向我们解释这一点。

00:11:30.299 --> 00:11:31.580
Like, no, no, no, lean into it.
就像是在说，不，不，不，顺应这种形式吧。

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:32.500
It has to be something new.
它必须是全新的东西。

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:38.659
I remember I was actually in New York once we got picked up and I got a call from Dana Walden.
我记得在我们剧集获批后，我其实正在纽约，然后我接到了达娜·沃尔登的电话。

00:11:38.659 --> 00:11:39.940
I stopped to get somebody to eat.
我正停下来买点东西吃。

00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:46.039
I got a call from her and we had a conversation about audience and no audience.
我接到了她的电话，我们聊了关于有观众和没有观众的事。

00:11:46.039 --> 00:11:53.399
And I said, if you want what you just saw, no audience and we'll make it work.
我说，如果你想要你刚刚看到的那种效果，那就不要现场观众，我们会把它做出来的。

00:11:53.399 --> 00:11:57.679
And she was on board bringing it back to those quiet moments and the emotional moments in the heart.
她很赞同把那种安静的时刻、情感的时刻和内心的触动带回来。

00:11:57.679 --> 00:12:05.240
I think that is a real positive side effect of not having a big bleacher full of 200 people to play to.
我觉得，不用面对满场坐着200人的大看台去表演，这是一个非常积极的副作用。

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:07.220
I really do think it makes a difference.
我真的觉得这大不一样。

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:08.059
Yeah.
是的。

00:12:08.059 --> 00:12:17.200
We've talked a lot, Pam, on the show, about that there was something, it was tough to be inside of because you couldn't depend.
帕姆，我们在节目里聊过很多次，说身处那种环境其实挺不容易的，因为你没有依仗。

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:19.559
You know, the crew would laugh once.
你知道的，剧组人员只会笑一次。

00:12:19.559 --> 00:12:21.399
They'd chuckle maybe again.
他们可能还会轻笑第二下。

00:12:21.399 --> 00:12:25.399
And you were just having to trust that you were landing your jokes.
你只能凭信念去相信你的笑话能逗笑观众。

00:12:25.399 --> 00:12:35.840
you had to almost hear the laughter like in your mind because the laughter was paped in afterwards, but I always maintain that it kept us from being overly amped up.
你几乎得在脑海里想象出笑声，因为笑声是事后后期录进去的，但我一直坚持认为，这让我们避免了表现得过于亢奋。

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:40.559
It kept us grounded in this almost like much more cinematic, realistic way.
它让我们保持了一种更接地气、更具电影感和现实感的方式。

00:12:40.559 --> 00:13:05.117
So when we got to, we just did nothing good happens after 2 a.m., which is such a good episode and such a darkly funny episode without a ton of jokes, but it's the kind of episode where you have 18 episodes behind it, like pulling the arrow back on the bow and it just sails, so it's got so much momentum.
所以当我们拍到《凌晨两点后无好事》这一集时——这是一集非常棒、黑色幽默十足且没有大量笑话的剧集，但它是那种经过了前面18集铺垫的剧集，就像把弓拉满一样，顺势而出，所以它有着极强的势头。

00:13:05.117 --> 00:13:20.899
But I think the kind of acting that's done between that me and Kobe were doing that me and Ashley were doing in that episode, it would be really hard to do that with 200 people eating sweet tarts.
但我认为，我和科比在、我和阿什利在该集中的那种表演，如果面对200个吃着甜心糖果的观众，是很难演出来的。

00:13:20.899 --> 00:13:24.120
It was just a different vibe on the set.
片场的氛围就是完全不同的。

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:24.755
you know.
你知道的。

00:13:24.755 --> 00:13:27.224
It was a completely different vibe.
那是一种截然不同的氛围。

00:13:27.224 --> 00:13:36.045
The jokes, to me, the jokes would never have been satisfying for an audience because they happened on the edit so many times.
对我来说，这些笑话如果面对现场观众是永远不会让人满意的，因为它们在剪辑中被调整了太多次。

00:13:36.045 --> 00:13:41.760
And it allowed us to have all of these sets and all of this time to get it right.
这让我们拥有了所有的布景和充足的时间来把事情做好。

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:46.558
And we always had great writers on stage as well as Cardone Cardone.
而且我们现场一直都有出色的编剧，卡尔顿和卡尔顿也在。

00:13:46.558 --> 00:13:47.828
You could feel it.
你能感受到这一点。

00:13:47.828 --> 00:13:52.133
And you guys were reacting to each other, which was so great.
你们俩当时在互相碰撞、互相回应，这太棒了。

00:13:52.133 --> 00:13:54.320
And we all knew when we had it.
我们大家在拍出好效果时心里都有数。

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:56.225
And it happened so quickly.
而且一切发生得那么快。

00:13:56.225 --> 00:14:00.459
It wasn't like we needed the first season to get the rhythm.
我们并不是非得用第一季来寻找节奏。

00:14:00.459 --> 00:14:02.717
It just happened so organically.
一切都是那么自然而然地发生了。

00:14:02.717 --> 00:14:11.820
And when you look at all of these episodes, because I've been watching with you guys, my God, that first season was unbelievable.
当你回看所有这些剧集时——因为我一直在和你们一起看——天哪，第一季简直不可思议。

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:12.525
It's good.
真的很棒。

00:14:12.525 --> 00:14:13.372
It holds up.
经得起时间的考验。

00:14:13.372 --> 00:14:15.559
It feels what it is right away.
一上来就找到了它应有的基调。

00:14:15.559 --> 00:14:17.489
There's some real bangers in that first season.
第一季里有几集真的是王炸级别的。

00:14:17.489 --> 00:14:20.279
We're pretty impressed with what we were able to do that for season.
我们对第一季能做到那种水平感到相当自豪。

00:14:20.279 --> 00:14:20.779
Yeah.
是的。

00:14:20.779 --> 00:14:55.244
Craig, can you just talk a little bit and maybe Pam too about the, it's not unheard of and I know, Pam, that you've actually done it yourself, but most television shows do not have a single director directing 99.9% of the shows that you have, maybe some in the first season and then you start cycling them in and the show has its own rhythm man in its own feel and so a new director has to be a part of that they are there they're serving that feel but this was a very very different situation can you talk a little bit about the choice to do that or Craig was it never a decision like Pam we just want you to stay forever please.
克雷格，你能简单聊聊吗？也许帕姆也可以聊聊——虽然这并非前所未有，而且我知道帕姆你其实自己也做过，但绝大多数电视剧都不会由单一导演执导99.9%的剧集，可能第一季会有一些导演，然后开始轮换，剧集有它自己的节奏和风格，所以新导演必须融入其中，他们来到现场，服务于这种风格，但这却是一个非常非常不同的情况。你能稍微聊聊当时做出这种选择的原因吗？还是说，克雷格，这根本就不是个决策，就像是“帕姆，我们只是希望你永远留下来，求你了”？

00:14:55.244 --> 00:15:03.833
Oh I mean it was a hundred percent that I mean I can't explain the going back to that Starbucks for a minute when Pam walked in it was just like we love her.
噢，我的意思是，百分之百就是这样。我的意思是，说回到那一分钟我们去星巴克的时候，当帕姆走进来，那种感觉就是我们太喜欢她了。

00:15:03.833 --> 00:15:10.726
We had heard every amazing thing about her that turns out to be true and then some when you really work with her and meet her.
我们之前听过的所有关于她的惊艳赞誉，等你真正和她共事、与她见面后，发现不仅完全属实，甚至有过之而无不及。

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:16.539
And then we, she was the in-demand, she was the bell of the ball every pilot season.
然后呢，她是炙手可热的人，每个试播集季里最耀眼的明星。

00:15:16.539 --> 00:15:18.419
And everyone was like, you're not gonna get her.
每个人都说：“你们请不到她的。”

00:15:18.419 --> 00:15:19.700
You're not gonna get her, you're not gonna get her.
“你们请不到她，你们请不到她。”

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:21.480
Pam has 20 other offers.
“帕姆还有20个其他的邀约呢。”

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:28.019
Why would she go with the script that like two 29-year-old guys who've never run a TV show wrote, maybe were just 30?
“她凭什么要选那个由两个从没制作过电视剧、29岁的小伙子写的剧本——也许当时刚满30岁？”

00:15:28.019 --> 00:15:32.620
Why would she choose what kind of idiot would she use that one to direct?
“她怎么会选那个，得有多傻才会选那个来执导？”

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:35.220
And it turned out, Pam was like...
结果呢，帕姆的反应是……

00:15:35.220 --> 00:15:37.298
Do you know what it is?
你知道这是为什么吗？

00:15:37.298 --> 00:15:49.860
It's another love story connected to this show because for me, and I fell in love and it turned out I didn't want to share it with anybody.
这又是与这部剧结缘的另一段爱情故事，因为对我来说，我坠入了爱河，结果发现我不想跟任何人分享它。

00:15:50.980 --> 00:15:53.434
And I wanted to be there every week.
我想每周都待在那里。

00:15:53.434 --> 00:15:57.797
I mean, every day driving onto that lot was the best experience.
我是说，每天开车驶入那个片场都是最棒的体验。

00:15:57.797 --> 00:16:35.700
I mean, it it it it was, you know, something you I think helped the whole cast and I'm and I'm sure Carter and Craig with was because you had had such a sampling of the many varieties of how TV shows can be how the what the egos can be like what the schedule can be like and you were constantly reminding us this is wonderful like I had less to compare it to you know and and it And it was really like you, there was this con, in addition to all the other many incredible things you were doing just professionally, just personally, you would pull us aside.
我的意思是，它，它，它，你知道的，我认为你对整个演员阵容——我相信卡特和克雷格也一样——都有很大帮助，因为你曾亲身体验过电视剧的各种不同制作方式，见过各种各样的人格有多自负、档期可以有多么繁琐，而你一直在提醒我们：“这太棒了。”（相比之下）我能比较的东西更少，你知道的，而且，而且真的就像你一样，除了你在专业上、个人生活上做的所有其他许多不可思议的事情之外，你还会把我们拉到一旁。

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:41.059
And it was always like, remember like to pinch ourselves, this is a really good job.
那种感觉总是像：提醒我们掐自己一下，这是一份非常好的工作。

00:16:41.059 --> 00:16:43.100
We have something very special here.
我们在这里拥有非常特别的东西。

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:53.340
And your presence and fidelity and dedication to the show, the fact that you didn't want to go anywhere when you could have been anywhere else was a signal to us like, oh, this is a good gig.
你的存在、你的忠诚以及对剧集的奉献，明明在别处有无数选择却哪里都不愿去的态度，对我们来说就是一个信号：噢，这是一份好工作。

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:59.940
is like something to really take seriously, there's something to value, this is something to just feel very grateful for.
这真的是一件需要认真对待、值得珍视的事情，是一件让人感到无比感恩的事情。

00:16:59.940 --> 00:17:03.659
And I'm grateful for you for helping me stay in that space.
我很感激你帮我保持了那种心态。

00:17:03.659 --> 00:17:12.619
It was lovely to be able to sort of parent the five of you, especially my friend Joshua in the beginning.
能够像长辈一样照顾你们五个人，尤其是刚开始时的我的朋友约书亚，感觉很美好。

00:17:12.619 --> 00:17:14.579
And me and Carter too, we were all the same age.
我和卡特也是，我们当时都差不多大。

00:17:14.579 --> 00:17:22.859
Yeah, but in the beginning Joshua, I remember it was so overwhelming to say, you're gonna be on a show, and people are gonna stop you in the street.
对，但在刚开始时，约书亚，我记得那种感觉太压抑了，（我们要对他说）你将出演一部电视剧，人们会在街上拦住你。

00:17:22.859 --> 00:17:25.019
It's a lot, it's a lot.
这压力太大了，太大了。

00:17:25.019 --> 00:17:48.940
Yeah, and I think especially, it was, you know, Kobe was new to it, but there was something, I don't know, there's something about Kobe, she's just a little to the man or born, you know, like she just kind of stepped into it a little more effortlessly than I did and Ali and Neil and Jason had had, they had had public things, they had had people stop them in airports and on the street, they were a little more used to that.
是的，而且我觉得尤其是，你知道的，科比虽然是新人，但有某种——我不知道，科比身上有一种特质，她简直就像是天生吃这碗饭的，你知道吗？就像她比我、比阿里、尼尔和杰森更加游刃有余地融入了这一切。他们过去都经历过公开露面，有过在机场和街上被人认出的经历，他们对那种情况稍微更习惯一些。

00:17:48.940 --> 00:17:51.140
This was my first go at this.
而这对我来说是第一次经历。

00:17:51.140 --> 00:18:04.539
This was my first, you know, when I did the graduate, I was famous on a half block of West 45th Street from, you know, 10 o'clock to 10, 15 every night, and then I rounded the corner and I was back to being anonymous.
这是我的第一次，你知道的，当年我演《毕业生》时，在西45街只有半个街区那么大范围里，每天晚上从10点到10点15分我是个名人，然后一拐过街角，我又变成了默默无闻的路人。

00:18:04.539 --> 00:18:14.339
So, you know, this was really new to me and it was very destabilizing, as you remember, and you were really, you couldn't have been more kind and loving to me throughout all that.
所以，你知道的，这对我来说真的很新鲜，而且非常让人心神不定，正如你所记得的那样，而你在经历这一切的过程中，对我给予了无比的善良和关爱。

00:18:14.339 --> 00:18:19.700
And also, I remember very strongly you telling me, this isn't going to give you everything you need artistically.
另外，我清楚地记得你曾对我说过：“这不会满足你艺术上的全部需求。”

00:18:19.700 --> 00:18:33.900
you're gonna have to do other stuff to supplement this wonderful, wonderful job, which really edged me into making my own movies and really creating this kind of multi-hyphenate life that I live now, today.
“你还得做点别的事来补充这份美好得无以复加的工作，”这番话实际上促使我去拍摄自己的电影，并真正开启了我如今所过的这种身兼多职的生活。

00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:37.539
So just endlessly grateful to you.
所以我对你感激不尽。

00:18:37.539 --> 00:18:39.900
You know how much I love you, but I'd like to say I'm already.
你知道我有多爱你，但我想说我已经准备好了。

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:42.740
And I'm wildly proud of you.
我对你感到无比的骄傲。

00:18:42.740 --> 00:18:44.900
Well, I felt undermined by you the whole time.
好吧，我感觉这一路上我一直在被你拆台。

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:45.819
That's good.
这挺好的。

00:18:45.819 --> 00:18:48.099
Do you remember, we talked about it.
你还记得吗，我们聊过这个。

00:18:48.099 --> 00:18:51.259
Do you remember meeting me?
你还记得我们初次见面吗？

00:18:51.259 --> 00:18:53.500
Let's talk about meeting me.
我们来聊聊初次见面吧。

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:54.500
You do.
你当然记得。

00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:55.500
I do.
我记得。

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:56.500
I do.
我记得。

00:18:56.500 --> 00:18:57.500
What was that like?
那是什么感觉？

00:18:57.500 --> 00:18:58.859
I remember that whole process.
我记得整个过程。

00:18:58.859 --> 00:19:15.430
It was just so fun to read something and then to meet you and go, yes, yes, because you lead with your heart, you always lead with your heart and it's talk about a tricky role to play.
读到剧本然后与你见面，那种感觉太有趣了，心想：就是他，就是他，因为你是用心在引领一切，你总是用心引领，而且这真是一个颇具挑战性的角色。

00:19:15.430 --> 00:19:25.049
Well, it was also, we've talked about it, but I was the first person on the first day of auditions, which must have been a little weird to go, wait, I think this is the guy, but we haven't seen any other guys, right?
嗯，这也是——我们聊过这个，我是第一天试镜时的第一个人，当时感觉一定有点诡异，心想：等等，我觉得这可能就是我们要找的人，但我们还没见过其他任何人呢，对吧？

00:19:25.049 --> 00:19:27.730
Gosh, I remember all of those auditions.
天哪，我记得所有那些试镜。

00:19:27.730 --> 00:19:34.730
I remember Neil coming in and throwing himself against the wall and all that crazy stuff.
我记得尼尔走进来，把自己往墙上撞以及所有那些疯狂的举动。

00:19:34.730 --> 00:19:46.401
Do you think we've been talking a little bit about, I think that sometimes we overvalue effort and we overvalue things like a struggle or I saw a thousand people before we found our person.
你觉得我们是不是一直在谈论——我觉得有时候我们过分看重努力，过分看重诸如挣扎之类的东西，或者“我们在找到合适的人选之前面试了成千上万个人”。

00:19:46.401 --> 00:19:55.294
Sometimes when things are right, you almost have to kick the tires a little bit to see because sometimes you're like, how could it be that easy?
有时候当一切都很顺利时，你几乎得去挑挑毛病、一探究竟，因为有时候你会想：怎么会这么容易？

00:19:55.294 --> 00:19:58.444
How could the cast just come together so perfectly?
演员阵容怎么能如此完美地凑到一起？

00:19:58.444 --> 00:20:00.173
How could Craig loves Buffy?
克雷格怎么会喜欢《吸血鬼猎人巴菲》？

00:20:00.173 --> 00:20:01.408
How did Ali say yes?
艾莉怎么会答应？

00:20:01.408 --> 00:20:06.596
There were just certain things that dropped into place in a somewhat effortless way.
有些事情就是以一种毫不费力的方式各就各位了。

00:20:06.596 --> 00:20:09.004
It can be hard, I think, to trust that.
我想，要相信这一点可能会很难。

00:20:09.004 --> 00:20:11.907
But I think in this case, it was to be trusted.
但我觉得在这个案子里，这是值得信任的。

00:20:11.907 --> 00:20:14.810
The right people signed on for the right roles.
对的人签约出演了对的角色。

00:20:14.809 --> 00:20:49.700
It was absolutely to be trusted and and what we what we learned was that the the effort was taking this gargantuan series and fitting it into three days Hats off to Michael Shea and Susie and everybody else, but that was to get it done was Was really something and and we did every week every week Yes, and thank you, Pam, for not being scared off by how ambitious the show was.
这绝对是值得信任的，我们学到的是，我们所付出的努力就是把这部庞大的剧集塞进三天里完成。向迈克尔·谢伊、苏西以及其他所有人致敬，但要把它拍完，真的太了不起了，而且我们每周都这么做，每周都如此。是的，谢谢帕姆，没有被这档节目的雄心壮志吓退。

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:56.700
It was written so recklessly and was such like a band-aid, like kind of guile-less, you know, abandon.
它的剧本写得太随性了，就像打补丁一样，带有一种没心没肺的、你知道的、随心所欲的劲儿。

00:20:56.700 --> 00:20:57.700
Like we didn't know any better.
就像我们什么都不懂一样。

00:20:57.700 --> 00:20:59.180
So we just wrote what we wanted to see.
所以我们只是写了我们想看的东西。

00:20:59.180 --> 00:21:02.980
You could have seen that and been like, who the hell are these guys and run screaming from it?
你本可以看了之后说：“这群人到底是谁啊？”然后尖叫着逃跑。

00:21:02.980 --> 00:21:06.900
Even after the pilot, like the pilot, four day big beast of a shoe, you're like, no, I want that challenge.
甚至在试播集之后，比如试播集，那是一个历时四天、难度极大的庞然大物，你会觉得：“不，我想要这个挑战。”

00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:07.900
Susie was the same way.
苏西也是这样。

00:21:07.900 --> 00:21:12.099
Our whole creative team and the cast were just up for the challenge.
我们的整个创意团队和演员阵容都欣然接受了挑战。

00:21:12.099 --> 00:21:13.099
And I think Josh was right.
而且我觉得乔希说得对。

00:21:13.099 --> 00:21:17.819
There was something effortless about it was like this script was its own little magnet that drew people to it.
这里面有一种不费吹灰之力Е的感觉，就像这个剧本本身就是一个小磁铁，把人们吸引了过来。

00:21:17.819 --> 00:21:21.220
It was bigger than Carter or me or any of us.
它比卡特、我或我们任何一个人都要宏大。

00:21:21.220 --> 00:21:24.619
And it was like, God, it just felt a little bit magical.
这就好像，天哪，感觉有点像魔法。

00:21:24.619 --> 00:21:25.619
I mean, it did.
我是说，确实如此。

00:21:25.619 --> 00:21:26.619
It felt magical.
感觉就像有魔法一样。

00:21:26.619 --> 00:21:28.099
It felt like it called the right people.
感觉它召唤了对的人。

00:21:28.099 --> 00:21:35.980
And you realize that, you know, nine years is a long time and there are always so many changes behind the scenes.
你会意识到，你知道的，九年是很长的时间，幕后总会有那么多的变动。

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:42.059
There were so few changes on our show, specifically on stage.
但我们的剧组，尤其是片场，变动太少了。

00:21:42.059 --> 00:21:47.859
couple people, you know, we lost a couple of people, but everybody wanted to be there.
少数几个人，你知道的，我们失去了几个人，但每个人都想留在那儿。

00:21:47.859 --> 00:21:48.859
Everybody.
每个人都想。

00:21:48.859 --> 00:21:52.019
And when there was something tricky to shoot, everybody's hand moored up.
每当有什么棘手的镜头要拍时，所有人的手都举了起来。

00:21:52.019 --> 00:21:53.019
Can I?
我可以吗？

00:21:53.019 --> 00:21:54.019
Let me do it.
让我来吧。

00:21:54.019 --> 00:21:55.019
Yeah.
好啊。

00:21:55.019 --> 00:21:56.019
Let me do it.
让我来。

00:21:56.019 --> 00:21:57.779
It was, it was like, we, you know, we owned it.
这就好像，我们，你知道的，我们把这当成了自己的事。

00:21:57.779 --> 00:21:58.779
We owned it.
我们全身心投入。

00:21:58.779 --> 00:22:09.619
This is just, I haven't thought about this in years, but Chris, was Chris camera a, not not Chris, uh, um, Chris Condi, yes, Chris Condi was X.
这只是……我好几年没想过这个了，但是克里斯，克里斯是A组摄影师吗？不对，不是克里斯，呃……克里斯·康迪，对，克里斯·康迪是X组。

00:22:09.619 --> 00:22:10.619
Yes.
对。

00:22:10.619 --> 00:22:12.059
She was next camera, yeah.
她当时是副摄影，对。

00:22:12.059 --> 00:22:19.380
So I remember when there were three cameras going and her camera wasn't on, she would always like frame up something really beautiful to look at.
所以我记得当有三个摄影机在拍而她的摄影机没开时，她总是会构图一些非常美丽的画面供人欣赏。

00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:20.420
Always.
总是这样。

00:22:20.420 --> 00:22:29.380
Like an incredible like a portrait or like a shot that you just, there was such like, she's literally like stepping away for a shot.
就像一个不可思议的肖像，或者一个你一眼看过去的镜头，那种感觉太……她实际上就像是退到一旁不去拍镜头一样。

00:22:29.380 --> 00:22:31.819
Like she doesn't need to be there because her camera is not on.
就像她不需要在那儿，因为她的摄影机没开。

00:22:31.819 --> 00:22:34.859
But she would give you guys something at the monitor to look at.
但她会给监视器前的你们一些好看的东西。

00:22:34.859 --> 00:22:41.480
And I always thought that was like, something so sweet and delicate and beautiful that didn't need to be done.
我一直觉得那是一件非常贴心、细腻且美好的事，其实完全没必要这么做。

00:22:41.480 --> 00:22:42.880
Pride of ownership.
对作品的骄傲感与主人翁意识。

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:43.380
Right?
对吧？

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:47.398
It was kind of like, we were trying to create an environment that was beautiful.
这有点像是，我们在努力营造一个美丽的环境。

00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:50.240
Because of you guys, everybody on that stage felt lucky.
因为有你们，片场里的每个人都觉得自己很幸运。

00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:52.559
Everybody felt lucky to be there.
每个人都觉得自己能身处其中是件很幸运的事。

00:22:52.559 --> 00:22:54.480
Right, right, right.
没错，没错，没错。

00:22:54.480 --> 00:23:02.119
Did you ever kick back like a Friday night, late at night and go, Jesus, I can't believe how much we shot in three days.
你有没有过在周五深夜，夜深人静的时候，往后一靠，然后说：“天哪，我真不敢相信我们在三天里拍了这么多。”

00:23:02.119 --> 00:23:04.960
Yeah, seriously, almost every Friday night.
对，说真的，几乎每个周五晚上都这样。

00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:06.799
And often every week, yeah.
而且通常每周都如此，是的。

00:23:06.799 --> 00:23:07.799
Yeah.
对。

00:23:07.799 --> 00:23:14.660
But you were busy reading the next script going, wait a music video, wait, yeah.
但你又忙着看下一集剧本，心里想着：“等等，还有音乐录影带？等等，好吧。”

00:23:14.660 --> 00:23:15.579
Where are we in a mall?
我们在商场里吗？

00:23:15.579 --> 00:23:21.539
We're in an 80s mall, but it's the 90s in Canada, ostensibly, like everything we did was so confusing.
我们在一个80年代的商场里，但在表面上它却是在90年代的加拿大，基本上，我们做的每件事都让人觉得匪夷所思。

00:23:21.539 --> 00:23:22.559
And you're like, yep, let's do it.
然后你会说：“好吧，开干吧。”

00:23:22.559 --> 00:23:24.059
We got this, we have a plan.
我们搞得定，我们有计划。

00:23:25.259 --> 00:23:26.579
Yeah, we could not scare you.
对，我们吓不倒你。

00:23:26.579 --> 00:23:27.779
We could not scare you away.
我们没能把你吓跑。

00:23:27.779 --> 00:23:31.940
And oh, how we tried, just so many times without our ambition.
而且天哪，我们是多么努力地想要吓跑你啊，仅仅是因为我们那过剩的野心。

00:23:31.940 --> 00:23:40.980
Are there any episodes, whether it's for season or from any season that you just kind of like are just top three moments on how I met your mother.
有没有哪几集，不管是哪一季的，让你觉得可以排进《老爸老妈浪漫史》里最棒的三个瞬间？

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:46.740
Like, I always think about us dancing in the rain for some reason after the hurricane episode.
比如，不知为什么，我总是想起我们在雨中跳舞的场景，就在飓风那集之后。

00:23:46.740 --> 00:23:52.220
I mean, there's so many moments, but drum roll please that you did not that long ago.
我是说，有太多的时刻了，但请敲起鼓点——这是你不久前才做过的。

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:54.650
I mean, can you beat that?
我是说，还有什么能比这更好？

00:23:54.650 --> 00:24:01.750
I remember you coming into my trailer and insisting that Ashley and I watch it after you got the cut.
我记得你走进我的拖车，坚持要我和阿什莉在你看完剪辑版后一起看。

00:24:01.750 --> 00:24:02.750
Isn't that funny?
这不是很有趣吗？

00:24:02.750 --> 00:24:03.589
Yes.
对的。

00:24:03.589 --> 00:24:04.430
Yeah, yeah.
对，对。

00:24:04.430 --> 00:24:27.660
There are way too many moments, really way too many moments to list, and that was part of what kept me there, there were constant moments where I would, as we were watching the katsu, it would be like, look at what we did, look at what we did.
精彩瞬间实在太多了，真的多到数不过来，这也是让我留在剧组的原因之一，总会有源源不断的时刻——当我们看着粗剪版时，会忍不住说：“看看我们做了什么，看看我们做了什么。”

00:24:27.660 --> 00:24:34.819
And it just talked me through this like, so would Sue do her own kind, like a rough assembly, right?
你给我讲讲这个流程吧，苏会先做一个粗剪版本，对吧？

00:24:34.819 --> 00:24:36.220
And then who would watch it next?
然后接下来是谁看？

00:24:36.220 --> 00:24:38.420
Would it be Carter and Craig would watch it next?
是卡特和克雷格接着看吗？

00:24:38.420 --> 00:24:39.539
Oh, that would be you.
哦，那得是你。

00:24:39.539 --> 00:24:42.420
So you'd get a director like a pass, like a director pass.
所以你会进行导演剪辑，就像导演剪辑版那样。

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:42.978
Exactly.
没错。

00:24:42.978 --> 00:24:47.380
And I would never call a Sufetterman cut rough man shoes, yeah.
我可从来不会把苏·费特曼的剪辑叫作粗剪，哥们儿，真的。

00:24:47.380 --> 00:24:49.180
The multiple Emmy winning Sufetter.
那位多次获得艾美奖的苏·费特曼。

00:24:49.180 --> 00:24:50.019
Unbelievable.
难以置信。

00:24:50.019 --> 00:24:53.019
But she had so much stuff to go through.
但她要处理的东西太多了。

00:24:53.019 --> 00:24:55.279
A lot of the time as we would talk beforehand.
很多时候，我们会在事前进行交流。

00:24:55.279 --> 00:24:59.240
so I could give her a heads up on, you know, certain things.
这样我可以就某些事情给她提个醒。

00:24:59.240 --> 00:25:02.000
But yeah, it was me and that it was the guys.
不过是的，就是我和她，然后还有这几个伙计。

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:04.519
And was a lot of that cutting or tweaking or?
那其中有很多剪辑或调整的工作吗？

00:25:04.519 --> 00:25:14.640
Yeah, yeah, it was, I actually did this, look for this shot, look for this, what if we do this, what if we pull this up or, you know, it's just, it's a director's cut.
对，对，确实有很多，我当时会做这些：“看这个镜头，看这个，如果我们这样做会怎样，如果我们把这个提上来，或者，”你知道的，这就是导演剪辑版。

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:19.640
Do you have weird, without Janet's like scripty book?
如果没有珍妮特的那本剧本记录册，你们会不会觉得怪怪的？

00:25:20.279 --> 00:25:28.720
Do you, remember there was that fame, I don't know if you guys know this, but there's that famous press conference after LeBron had a game and someone asked him about a shot.
你们记得那个著名的名场面吗？我不知道你们知不知道，但勒布朗在打完一场比赛后有个著名的记者会，有人问他关于某个投篮的问题。

00:25:28.720 --> 00:25:36.799
And he said, well, you have to go back to, and he went back like eight plays in the game, and he described everything that happened up and down the court for like eight plays.
他说：“嗯，你得回溯到……”然后他一口气倒叙了比赛中的八个回合，描述了这八个回合中球场上下发生的每一件事。

00:25:36.799 --> 00:25:40.000
And you realize, oh, he's an actual genius of basketball.
然后你会意识到，哦，他真的是个篮球天才。

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:46.279
Like he has photographic memory of every play leading up to the whatever play they're asking about.
就像他对他们被问到的任何一个进球之前的所有战术都有摄影般的记忆。

00:25:46.279 --> 00:25:48.039
And did you have that kind of recall?
你们也有这种回忆能力吗？

00:25:48.039 --> 00:25:53.559
Like if you would see something, I would have this in movies where that I directed where I go, no, no, no.
比如如果你看到什么东西——我在我导演的电影里就会这样，我会说：“不，不，不，我记得莉齐·奥尔森左眼流下了一颗单薄的泪珠，我们必须把它找出来，我知道它就在里面。”

00:25:53.559 --> 00:25:57.200
I remember Lizzy Olson had a single teardrop down the left eye.
（注：前文同义合并，此处为上一句延续）

00:25:57.319 --> 00:25:58.319
We have to find it.
（注：前文同义合并，此处为上一句延续）

00:25:58.319 --> 00:25:59.240
I know it's in there.
（注：前文同义合并，此处为上一句延续）

00:25:59.240 --> 00:26:02.319
Did you have that where you would would remember?
你们也会有这种能记住细节的时候吗？

00:26:02.559 --> 00:26:04.000
Absolutely.
当然。

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:05.680
Don't ask, don't ask me now.
现在别问我，现在别问。

00:26:05.680 --> 00:26:10.359
But at the time, absolutely, because you, you do, you're sitting there.
但在当时绝对有，因为你会坐在那儿。

00:26:10.359 --> 00:26:11.359
You're watching everything.
你注视着一切。

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:13.079
I've written things down.
我都写下来了。

00:26:13.079 --> 00:26:15.240
I've taken notes and I'm so in it.
我做了笔记，而且完全沉浸其中。

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:24.680
But now when I'm watching these shows, as, as you guys are going through them, I'm like, oh my God, I felt so much, I have forgotten.
但现在当我看着这些节目，随着你们一起回顾时，我想，天哪，我感触太深了，我都忘了。

00:26:24.680 --> 00:26:29.279
And yet, I remember where the sets were on the stage, like the placement.
然而，我还记得舞台上布景的位置，比如它们的摆放。

00:26:29.279 --> 00:26:38.400
It's like, you know, it's, yes, the answer to that question is yes, at the time, for sure.
这就好像，你知道的，是的，那个问题的答案是肯定的，当时绝对是这样。

00:26:38.400 --> 00:26:39.240
Yeah.
是啊。

00:26:39.240 --> 00:26:43.319
Pam, one of your superpowers, I think, was making the cast feel so safe, I think.
帕姆，我觉得你的超能力之一，就是能让演员们感到非常安心。

00:26:43.319 --> 00:26:46.319
And also making Carter and I feel safe to try stuff.
而且也能让我和卡特感到安心去尝试各种东西。

00:26:46.319 --> 00:26:50.359
And again, you were one of the earliest votes for this pilot.
再说一次，你是这个试播集最早的投票支持者之一。

00:26:50.359 --> 00:26:55.119
You were one of the earliest people who put their hand up and said, yes, this will work at a time when we didn't know what we were doing.
你是最早举手说“这行得通”的人之一，而在当时，我们甚至不知道自己在做什么。

00:26:55.119 --> 00:26:56.599
You gave us a lot of confidence.
你给了我们很大的信心。

00:26:56.599 --> 00:26:59.359
Did you, how do you, how do you do that?
你，你是怎么做到这一点的？

00:26:59.359 --> 00:27:00.680
It's just a natural talent of yours.
这简直是你与生俱来的天赋。

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:09.599
Like you had the ability with these five new people in this show, and we even talked about this with Ashley Williams, kind of parachuting in in the middle of the season, needing to join a cast.
就像在这个节目中，面对这五个新人时，你有这种能力，我们甚至和阿什利·威廉姆斯谈过这种感觉——有点像在季中空降加入一个演员阵容。

00:27:09.599 --> 00:27:14.200
You had such an intuition of who needed to hear what when, who needed encouragement.
你有着惊人的直觉，知道谁在什么时候需要听什么话，谁需要鼓励。

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:18.039
If you had to dial something back, is that just like, can that be taught?
如果你需要让某人收敛一点，这，这能教得会吗？

00:27:18.039 --> 00:27:21.940
You just have this like a very empathetic, intuitive sense of people, I think.
我觉得你对人有一种非常富有同理心、极其敏锐的直觉。

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:22.940
I'm a mom.
我是个妈妈。

00:27:23.940 --> 00:27:27.640
I mean, that's kind of a lot of it.
我是说，很大程度上是因为这个。

00:27:27.640 --> 00:27:28.640
And I really didn't.
而且我真的没有……

00:27:29.539 --> 00:27:31.440
I mean, that's who I am.
我是说，这就是我。

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:33.140
That's how I approach things.
这就是我处理事情的方式。

00:27:33.140 --> 00:27:42.140
I'm, I guess I nurture and I really, I know it sounds so tried, but I, I loved these five act.
我，我想我是有养育和包容的天性，我知道这听起来很陈词滥调，但我，我真的很爱这五个演员。

00:27:42.140 --> 00:27:46.539
I, I, they're a part of me and we fell and I want to take care of them.
我，他们是我的一部分，我们一同经历低谷，而我想照顾他们。

00:27:46.539 --> 00:27:58.539
And I do believe that if you nurture that kind of a relationship and they know that they are seen and heard and valued, they want to be there.
而且我相信，如果你悉心维系这种关系，让他们知道自己被看到、被听到、被重视，他们就会想要留在这里。

00:27:58.539 --> 00:28:00.721
They want to do their best work.
他们会想要展现出最好的一面。

00:28:00.721 --> 00:28:01.539
All of that.
所有这些。

00:28:01.539 --> 00:28:04.317
And I mean that for everybody.
我的意思是，这对所有人都适用。

00:28:04.317 --> 00:28:06.539
It is such a team sport.
这真是一项团队运动。

00:28:06.539 --> 00:28:13.799
And it's just such a lucky seat to be in for me.
对我来说，能坐在这样一个位置上真是太幸运了。

00:28:13.799 --> 00:28:14.299
me.
我。

00:28:14.299 --> 00:28:23.481
I can't tell you how grateful I am and people are still coming up to me to say great things about this show.
我无法表达我有多感激，人们到现在还会跑来对我说这部节目的好话。

00:28:23.481 --> 00:28:27.137
It changed my life in such a beautiful way.
它以如此美好的方式改变了我的人生。

00:28:27.137 --> 00:28:37.084
It's just, and here we are together in 2025 because we still love each other and because what we did still resonates.
就是这样，而我们在2025年齐聚一堂，因为我们依然彼此相爱，因为我们所做的事依然能引起共鸣。

00:28:36.839 --> 00:28:39.720
I mean, my god, how lucky is that?
我的天哪，这是多大的幸运啊？

00:28:39.720 --> 00:28:49.660
Had this old man, he musted me, fell in love with you, new York City.
有个老头，他一定……我爱上了你，纽约市。

00:28:49.660 --> 00:28:51.700
And now, commercials!
好了，广告时间！

00:28:51.700 --> 00:28:58.529
Hi, I'm Alex Goldman, host of the Hyper-Fixed Podcast.
嗨，我是亚历克斯·戈尔德曼，《Hyper-Fixed》播客的主持人。

00:28:58.529 --> 00:29:02.250
Each week, we take listeners' problems and try to solve them for them.
每周，我们都会倾听听众的烦恼并试着为他们解决。

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:05.210
Problems like, I'm 30 and I'm scared to drive in New York.
比如这样的问题：我30岁了，但害怕在纽约开车。

00:29:05.210 --> 00:29:08.890
Or, why can't I adjust the volume of my car stereo when I'm in reverse?
或者，为什么我在倒车时不能调节车载音响的音量？

00:29:08.890 --> 00:29:11.410
We also solve non-car related problems.
我们也解决与汽车无关的问题。

00:29:11.410 --> 00:29:17.519
If you have a problem, not only will we fix it, we'll expose the hidden systems that cause that problem in the first place.
如果你有什么烦恼，我们不仅会帮你解决，还会揭开导致该问题的幕后隐藏机制。

00:29:17.519 --> 00:29:20.000
That's the HyperFixed Podcast from Radiotopia.
这就是来自Radiotopia的《Hyper-Fixed》播客。

00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:23.359
Find it wherever you find podcasts, or at HyperFixedPod.com.
请在各大播客平台或访问 HyperFixedPod.com 收听。

00:29:34.819 --> 00:29:38.259
And of commercials, back to Shell.
广告结束，言归正传。

00:29:38.259 --> 00:29:59.700
Well, we're so constantly struck by the meta kind of quality of we were making a show about a guy's memories about these very special times that imprinted on his heart, these moments that were worth remembering and worth telling about and worth passing onto his kids.
嗯，我们经常为这种“戏中戏”的元叙事特质所打动：我们当时在制作一个关于某个男人的回忆的节目，关于那些铭刻在他心头的特殊时光，那些值得铭记、值得讲述、值得传给孩子们的时刻。

00:29:59.700 --> 00:30:05.940
But we ourselves were imprinting these memories of the making of this show upon our heart at the same time.
但与此同时，我们自己也将制作这部节目的回忆深深铭刻在了心头。

00:30:05.940 --> 00:30:13.099
Like it exists on so many different strange levels of meaning what this show is.
就像这部节目在如此多奇异的意义层面上存在着。

00:30:13.099 --> 00:30:42.859
And I just wanna say vis-a-vis, You know like the way you needed to talk to the all five of us those are five different languages Like you didn't talk to me and Neil the same way you didn't talk to Ali and Jason the same way Like they're there everyone requires a different kind of handling or needs to be seen or appreciated in a different way Including the crew including every single person on that set and I did think that you were You have and this is not something that can be taught.
我只想说，就比如说……你知道的，你需要跟我们五个人沟通的方式，那是五种不同的语言。比如你跟我说话的方式和对尼尔的方式不同，你对阿里和杰森说话的方式也不同。就好像，每个人都需要不同方式的关照，或者需要以不同的方式被看到、被欣赏。包括剧组人员，包括片场的每一个人。我真的觉得你拥有……而且这不是能教出来的。你无法为此获得一个艺术硕士学位，你知道的，比如如何看懂一个房间的气氛，如何看懂一个人。

00:30:42.859 --> 00:30:47.900
You can't get an MFA in this you know, like how to read a room, how to read people.
你无法为此获得一个艺术硕士学位，你知道的，比如如何看懂现场气氛，如何看懂人。

00:30:47.900 --> 00:30:58.940
And I just thought it's one of your real superpowers of helping people feel seen and heard so that they can do their best work, because we've all had experiences where we don't feel that.
我只是觉得，这是你的超能力之一，能帮助人们感到自己被看到、被听到，从而让他们发挥出最好的水平，因为我们都有过没有这种感受的经历。

00:30:58.940 --> 00:31:03.700
And I clench up, I get like, I can't actually do my best work.
而我会变得紧张，我会觉得……实际上无法发挥出最好的水平。

00:31:03.700 --> 00:31:08.259
So I'm just so grateful to you for that.
所以我为此真的非常感激你。

00:31:08.259 --> 00:31:18.740
I did wanna ask you, directing a show like this or even directing more generally, is, I don't think a lot of people understand it, generally directing, I don't know.
我确实想问你，导演这样一部剧，或者甚至更广泛地谈导演工作，我觉得很多人其实并不真正理解，广义上的导演工作，我不知道。

00:31:18.740 --> 00:31:19.579
I don't know.
我不知道。

00:31:19.579 --> 00:31:24.579
I don't know a lot of people understand what the job is.
我不知道有多少人真正理解这项工作是什么。

00:31:25.019 --> 00:31:29.420
Do you have a way to describe your job or how do you describe it?
你有什么方式来描述你的工作，或者你是怎么描述的？

00:31:29.420 --> 00:31:35.180
Like when people who really don't know what it is, like what would you say you do?
当那些完全不懂的人问你时，你会说自己是做什么的？

00:31:35.180 --> 00:31:44.500
I get it done, I get it from the script to the TV, in a very general sense.
我把事情搞定，从最宏观的意义上讲，我把它从剧本搬上了电视。

00:31:44.500 --> 00:31:48.539
General sense, for all of you letting it up here.
宏观地讲，献给在座的各位。

00:31:48.539 --> 00:31:53.539
You know, I'm sure you've talked about how the week works.
你知道的，我确信你们讨论过一周的工作流程是怎样的。

00:31:53.940 --> 00:32:00.539
You know, you read it, you have a production meeting, you know, whatever, we rehearse it and we fine tune it, we show it to everybody.
你知道的，你阅读剧本，开制作会议，你知道的，无论如何，我们排练并进行微调，然后把它展示给所有人。

00:32:00.539 --> 00:32:18.460
And then more than anything with the help of a spectacular AD, that team, we sit down and figure out how do we get all of this done in this amount of time, and we move things around, which is one of the trickiest parts.
然后，在一位出色的副导演和团队的帮助下，最重要的是，我们坐下来想办法在规定时间内完成所有这些工作，我们四处调整，这是最棘手的部分之一。

00:32:18.460 --> 00:32:19.299
Yeah.
对。

00:32:19.299 --> 00:32:34.019
And then we say action, and we, for me, as you know, always looking at my watch, as well as looking at the monitor, very aware of what the job is.
然后我们喊开始，而对我来说，正如你们所知，我总是看着手表，同时也看着监视器，对这项工作有着高度的觉知。

00:32:34.019 --> 00:32:38.819
I can't at the end of the week say, oh, sorry, we didn't get all of that accomplished.
我不能在周末时说，哦，对不起，我们没能完成所有这些。

00:32:38.819 --> 00:33:07.960
So I was very aware of the restraints of the schedule, but because we didn't have to worry about an audience and because we had such a great freedom to explore when we were allowed to talk to each other, to talk to cameras and to get everybody to be a part of it, to be able to say to a camera operator, here's what I'm thinking, what do you think?
所以我对时间的限制有着清醒的认识，但因为我们不必担心现场观众，而且因为我们拥有极大的自由去探索——当我们被允许互相交流、与摄像机对话、让每个人都参与进来时——能够对摄像师说，“我是这么想的，你觉得呢？”

00:33:07.960 --> 00:33:12.400
Like that's a great, you don't get to do that on a normal show, there's no time.
这太棒了，你在一般的剧组里可没有这种待遇，根本没时间。

00:33:12.400 --> 00:33:21.880
But there's somebody who's behind that camera who sees things in a certain way, I do too, but so do they, let's get their take on this.
但那个摄像机背后的人以某种方式看待事物，我也是，但他们也是，让我们听听他们的看法。

00:33:21.880 --> 00:33:36.119
And we got to sort of empower everybody on that stage in the most profound way to me, to get everybody to step up and take ownership of what we're creating.
我们以一种对我来说极其深刻的方式，赋权给舞台上的每一个人，让每个人都挺身而出，对我们所创造的东西产生归属感。

00:33:36.119 --> 00:33:38.000
So we get to do all of that.
所以我们得以做到这一切。

00:33:38.000 --> 00:33:44.640
You get to check these, you know, crossing off, you know, giving you, giving you notes by the end of it.
你可以检查这些，你知道的，打勾，你知道的，最后给你提意见。

00:33:44.640 --> 00:33:47.720
I was texting Jason from my chair because everybody had their phones.
我坐在椅子上给杰森发短信，因为每个人都有手机。

00:33:47.720 --> 00:33:49.960
I was like, did you do this and this in?
我当时想：你把这个和那个拍进去了吗？

00:33:49.960 --> 00:33:51.920
And you just see him look at him and go.
然后你就会看到他看着你，然后点头。

00:33:53.789 --> 00:33:57.950
It was just so, it was just so magical.
这真是太神奇了，简直太神奇了。

00:33:58.950 --> 00:34:03.150
But get it done and we got it done, we got it done.
总之就是搞定它，我们做到了，我们搞定了。

00:34:03.150 --> 00:34:06.109
You know, in grad school, I worked with a wonderful director named Ken Washington.
你知道，在研究生院时，我和一位名叫肯·华盛顿的优秀导演合作过。

00:34:06.109 --> 00:34:13.099
And he told me that when he was younger, as a director, he thought all the good ideas had to come from him or they didn't count.
他告诉我，他年轻时作为一名导演，认为所有好的创意都必须来自他自己，否则就不算数。

00:34:13.099 --> 00:34:16.820
And as he got older, he realized good ideas can come from anywhere.
而随着年龄增长，他意识到好的创意可以来自任何地方。

00:34:16.820 --> 00:34:18.500
And they should come from anywhere.
并且它本就应该来自任何地方。

00:34:18.500 --> 00:34:36.059
You're expanding your pallet so much more if you ask the camera op, what do you think is the best way to get this shot, but it also to take the architecture metaphor, it feels like Carter and Craig and the writing staff hand you a blueprint, right?
如果你问摄像师觉得用什么方式拍这个镜头最好，你的创作视野就会极大地拓宽，但这也让我想起建筑学的比喻——感觉卡特、克莱尔和编剧团队交给你一份蓝纸，对吧？

00:34:36.059 --> 00:34:44.519
and you're on some level, tell me if you agree with this or not, like a bit of the contractor, like you have to figure out how to build the building from the thing they gave you.
从某种程度上来说——告诉我你是否同意这一点——你有点像个总承包商，你必须想办法根据他们给你的东西把这栋建筑建造起来。

00:34:44.519 --> 00:34:48.639
They give you the specs, they give you, you know, and you have to figure out how do we bring this into form?
他们给你规格说明，他们给你，你知道的，而你必须弄清楚我们如何把这个具象化？

00:34:48.639 --> 00:34:50.099
How do we bring this into reality?
我们如何把它变成现实？

00:34:50.099 --> 00:34:51.099
Does that resonate?
这有共鸣吗？

00:34:51.099 --> 00:34:58.119
It does, but the beauty is that the architects don't go away and that every day we're meeting.
有的，但美妙之处在于建筑师并没有走开，我们每天都在开会。

00:34:58.119 --> 00:35:02.960
So that when I put something up and show it to them, we can tweak it.
所以当我把某个东西摆出来展示给他们看时，我们可以对它进行调整。

00:35:02.960 --> 00:35:08.239
And because it's like detective work really, They've written this script upstairs.
因为这真的很像侦探工作。他们在楼上写了这个剧本。

00:35:08.239 --> 00:35:10.559
We haven't seen it because we're working on something else.
我们没看过，因为我们在忙别的事。

00:35:10.559 --> 00:35:16.280
And we get it and all of a sudden we're like, okay, here's what I think you mean.
我们拿到手，然后突然之间我们就会想，好吧，我觉得你们的意思是这样的。

00:35:16.280 --> 00:35:18.840
Here's what I think will tell the story.
我觉得这样拍能讲好这个故事。

00:35:18.840 --> 00:35:23.440
And then the beauty is I get to do that in turn around and go, guys, what do you think?
然后美妙之处就在于，我转过头去问：“伙计们，你们觉得呢？”

00:35:23.440 --> 00:35:25.519
Is this, are we on the same page?
“这个行吗，我们的想法一致吗？”

00:35:25.519 --> 00:35:29.079
And most of the time we work, but sometimes they were like, how about this?
大部分时候我们配合得很好，但有时他们会说：“这样怎么样？”

00:35:29.079 --> 00:35:32.159
And again, collaboration, collaboration.
再说一次，这就是合作，这就是协作。

00:35:32.159 --> 00:35:33.480
Yeah, it was so clever.
是的，太聪明了。

00:35:33.480 --> 00:35:34.840
All the writers felt that way, Pam.
所有编剧都这么觉得，帕姆。

00:35:34.840 --> 00:35:38.360
And I don't think that's necessarily how writers always feel about directors.
而且我觉得这未必是编剧对导演的一贯看法。

00:35:38.360 --> 00:35:44.519
If I may be so bold, like, you were just so welcoming and open to trying stuff.
如果我可以斗胆直说的话，就是，你当时是如此热情欢迎并且愿意尝试各种新东西。

00:35:44.519 --> 00:35:49.920
And we could, we would sit, you would have the writers sit right around you and just watch, and watch takes.
我们可以，我们可以坐着，你会让编剧们坐在你身边，看着，看着拍摄镜头。

00:35:49.920 --> 00:35:53.760
And there's either that quad split, the four shots, four different cameras on a big TV screen.
大电视屏幕上要么是那种四分画面，四个镜头，四个不同的摄像机。

00:35:53.760 --> 00:35:54.760
And you would.
而你就会。

00:35:54.760 --> 00:35:56.159
You would turn to us and say, what do you think?
你就会转向我们说，你们觉得怎么样？

00:35:56.159 --> 00:35:58.480
Or if we had a pitch, you'd say, let's give that a try.
或者如果我们有什么想法，你会说，我们试一试吧。

00:35:58.480 --> 00:36:02.119
And then when you would go in and try and it wouldn't work, you wouldn't make us feel like assholes or anything.
然后当你们进去尝试却行不通的时候，你也不会让我们觉得自己像个白痴什么的。

00:36:02.119 --> 00:36:03.119
You'd be like, all right, we tried that.
你会说，好吧，我们试过了。

00:36:03.119 --> 00:36:04.119
But that's enough.
但这就够了。

00:36:04.119 --> 00:36:04.960
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
对，对，对。

00:36:04.960 --> 00:36:09.480
And if it did work, you'd be like, that's great and also this other idea.
如果真的行得通，你就会说，太棒了，还有另外这个想法。

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:12.920
And it was so safe of a creative space.
这是一个极具安全感的创作空间。

00:36:12.920 --> 00:36:14.639
We talk about this a lot on the podcast.
我们在播客里经常讨论这个。

00:36:14.639 --> 00:36:18.440
I just don't believe in fear leading to good work.
我就是不相信恐惧能带来好作品。

00:36:18.440 --> 00:36:27.519
And there's this funny misconception, I think, in a lot of endeavors of all different shades and corporate, artistic, whatever.
我认为在许多不同领域的各种事务中，无论是企业、艺术还是其他什么，都有这样一个有趣的误区。

00:36:27.519 --> 00:36:38.119
And I think this is the tone you set on stage when we're creating something is proof positive that people feel valued and safe to pitch things and safe to try things.
我认为这就是你在片场我们创作时定下的基调，它绝对是强有力的证明，让大家觉得自己的价值被认可，可以安全地提出想法，安全地去尝试。

00:36:38.119 --> 00:36:41.199
And everyone's idea could get on and could be the best idea.
每个人的想法都可能被采纳，并成为最好的点子。

00:36:41.199 --> 00:36:42.360
It doesn't matter who.
是谁提出的并不重要。

00:36:42.360 --> 00:36:43.960
That's a really wonderful thing.
这真的是一件很棒的事。

00:36:43.960 --> 00:36:49.480
And that was really important as young writers who had not run their own TV show to feel with the director.
作为尚未独立制作过自己电视剧的年轻编剧，能从导演身上感受到这一点，真的非常重要。

00:36:49.480 --> 00:36:57.679
I don't know what we would have done if after that pilot you were like, I've got to go take these other 175 offers I have and left us.
我不知道如果拍完那集试播集之后，你就说“我得去接手我手头另外175个邀约了”然后抛下我们会怎么样。

00:36:57.679 --> 00:37:09.400
And we'd had to sort of figure it out week to week with other directors and maybe not had that same dynamic, and the cast wouldn't have had that same feeling of safety and playfulness and curiosity.
那我们就得每周跟不同的导演去摸索，可能就不会有那种默契，演员们也不会有那种安全感、趣味性和好奇心。

00:37:09.400 --> 00:37:11.239
I think it's a different show.
我觉得那会是另一部剧了。

00:37:11.239 --> 00:37:13.239
I just think it's a completely different show.
我只是觉得那是完全不同的一部剧。

00:37:13.239 --> 00:37:17.000
Yeah, but don't forget that you all made me better.
对，但别忘了，是你们所有人让我变得更好。

00:37:17.000 --> 00:37:20.239
You pushed me, it wasn't one-sided.
你们鞭策了我，这不是单方面的。

00:37:20.239 --> 00:37:23.119
I mean, I- I don't want to fight about this camp.
我是说，我——我不想为这个争论，伙计。

00:37:23.119 --> 00:37:25.079
It's you, it's all you.
全靠你，全都是因为你。

00:37:25.079 --> 00:37:26.159
After all these years.
历经这么多年。

00:37:26.159 --> 00:37:27.760
After all these years.
历经这么多年。

00:37:27.760 --> 00:37:28.260
You did.
你确实做了。

00:37:28.260 --> 00:37:29.082
You pushed me.
你鞭策了我。

00:37:29.082 --> 00:37:30.198
You made me better.
你让我变得更好。

00:37:30.198 --> 00:37:34.485
You forced me into uncomfortable situations and I had to figure them out.
你把我逼进不舒服的境地，而我必须去解决它们。

00:37:34.485 --> 00:37:38.361
There was a lot of painting ourselves in the corners wasn't there.
有很多把自己逼入绝境的情况，不是吗。

00:37:38.361 --> 00:37:41.591
There was a lot of like, how do we get out of this one?
有很多类似这样的情况：我们怎么摆脱这个困境？

00:37:41.591 --> 00:37:51.341
Well, it's a there's a great quote from some sculptor says, your life must be you have to ask yourself a question that you spend your whole life trying to figure out.
嗯，某个雕塑家有一句很棒的名言，他说，你的人生必须是，你必须问自己一个问题，然后花一辈子去试图弄明白它。

00:37:51.341 --> 00:37:55.041
And the only caveat is you must never be able to figure it out.
唯一的条件是，你绝对不能把它弄明白。

00:37:55.041 --> 00:38:08.138
Like, There's something about an insoluble question or unsolvable question that it's like as artists, like if something's too easy, it's almost like a difficulty of a puzzle or something like if it's too easy, it's not fun.
就像，某种关于无法解答的问题或难以解决的问题，作为艺术家，如果某件事太简单了，就像拼图的难度一样，如果太简单了就不好玩了。

